# TODO: bring benchmarks in line with the research spec

## Core execution model

Status: done.

- Removed StarPU-only comparison modes from the main benchmark matrix:
  - removed `starpu_cpu`;
  - removed `starpu_gpu`;
  - kept only `native_cpu`, `native_gpu`, `starpu_hybrid`.
- StarPU is now represented as a heterogeneous scheduler mode, not as a wrapper around CPU-only/GPU-only execution.
- Updated `ExecutionMode`, CLI help, README, scripts, and experiment matrix accordingly.

## Native baselines

- Add `native_cpu` and `native_gpu` implementations for every main scenario:
  - `matmul`;
  - `independent`;
  - `heterogeneous`;
  - `image`.
- Native CPU implementations should be simple and understandable, preferably scalar/linear loops where possible.
- Native GPU implementations should use straightforward CUDA kernels with simple data parallelism.
- Avoid highly optimized native baselines that make the comparison about library tuning instead of scheduling.

## Matrix multiplication

- Replace the current monolithic StarPU matmul task with a blocked/tiled implementation.
- Submit many independent block tasks so StarPU can actually distribute work between CPU and GPU.
- Use matrix sizes from the plan:
  - 512x512;
  - 1024x1024;
  - 2048x2048;
  - optionally larger sizes if memory and runtime allow.
- Consider whether to replace BLAS/cuBLAS with simpler CPU/GPU kernels for methodological consistency.
- Record tile size and number of submitted tasks in JSON metrics.

## Independent tasks

- Replace the current matvec-based workload with a simpler independent array-processing workload, for example:
  `y[i] = sin(x[i]) + sqrt(x[i]) + x[i]^2`.
- Each task should process a separate block/array and be independent from all other tasks.
- Add all three main modes:
  - native CPU sequential or simple threaded baseline if explicitly needed;
  - native GPU simple kernel over all blocks;
  - StarPU hybrid with one task per block.
- Vary task count to study scheduler behavior under different parallelism levels.
- Record task count, block size, total elements, and average task time.

## Heterogeneous workload

- Redesign the scenario around multiple task classes:
  - light tasks: small array processing;
  - medium tasks: medium array processing or small matrix operation;
  - heavy tasks: larger array processing or larger matrix operation.
- Add native CPU and native GPU baselines.
- Keep StarPU hybrid as the only StarPU mode.
- Submit tasks simultaneously to create uneven load and test dynamic balancing.
- Record counts per task class, sizes per class, and aggregate runtime.

## Image / edge-oriented scenario

- Keep image tiling, because it matches the edge-oriented scenario well.
- Replace or extend operations to match the plan:
  - blur;
  - edge detection;
  - convolution;
  - filtering.
- Fix current GPU blur behavior: it currently falls back to grayscale and is not a real blur.
- Fix native CPU image path so it uses a normal CPU function, not a StarPU buffer accessor.
- For convolution/blur on tiles, handle borders/halo regions correctly or explicitly document simplified borders.
- Add native CPU and native GPU paths for all selected operations.
- Record image size, tile size, operation, tile count, and possibly number of images if batch processing is added.

## Metrics and output files

- Every benchmark run must write a machine-readable result file.
- JSON result files should include:
  - scenario;
  - mode;
  - input parameters;
  - total runtime;
  - task count;
  - block/tile size;
  - StarPU-specific overhead fields when applicable;
  - path to system metrics CSV;
  - path/prefix to StarPU trace files when profiling is enabled.
- System metrics CSV should be linked to the benchmark JSON through a shared run id or explicit file path.
- Update `run_experiment.sh` so it passes or records the metrics CSV path in the result metadata.

## StarPU overhead

- Reconsider the standalone `bench_overhead` scenario.
- The current noop/memcpy benchmark is useful as a microbenchmark for task submission and data movement overhead.
- It is not fully representative of overhead inside real workloads, because real scenarios include computation, tiling, data dependencies, and CPU/GPU scheduling decisions.
- Prefer measuring overhead inside each main test case:
  - native total time vs StarPU hybrid total time for comparable work;
  - StarPU initialization time;
  - data registration time;
  - task submission time;
  - wait/synchronization time;
  - data unregister/copy-back time.
- Keep `bench_overhead` only as an auxiliary microbenchmark, not as a replacement for per-scenario overhead analysis.

## Remove unused benchmark

- Remove `hello_starpu`.
- Delete it from:
  - CMake target list;
  - README;
  - run scripts;
  - experiment matrix.
- It is useful only as a sanity check and does not contribute to the research evaluation.

## Documentation

- Rewrite README around the final three modes:
  - `native_cpu`;
  - `native_gpu`;
  - `starpu_hybrid`.
- Update integration notes so they distinguish:
  - implementation complexity;
  - scheduling overhead;
  - data movement overhead;
  - limitations of each scenario.
- Document any hardware/platform deviations separately from benchmark methodology.
